# The golden fixtures are compared byte for byte, so a clone that
# rewrote their line endings would fail every journey.
tests/golden/*.txt -text

# The same reasoning, one directory further out. With no `text`
# attribute git falls back to core.autocrlf, which is true by default in
# Git for Windows -- so eleven wholly-CRLF files (ai.c battle.c events.c
# finish.c moving.c planets.c reports.c setup.c sst.c sst.doc sst.h)
# would flip on checkout and flip back on commit. Measured on a clone of
# 13a7fb8 with core.autocrlf=true: `git add --renormalize .` rewrites
# exactly those eleven, 9,751 insertions and 9,751 deletions -- every
# line of all eleven, which is what `wc -l` over them gives, and 19,502
# lines to read in a diff. `-text` means git converts nothing in either
# direction, so a clone on any platform gets the bytes that were
# committed.
#
# These globs also freeze the newer LF files -- tui.c, tui.h, tuifmt.c,
# rules.c, rules.h, osx.c, osx.h and the two under tests/. That is
# deliberate rather than inherited: it is the same guarantee in the
# other direction, it covers any file added later without anyone
# remembering to, and a per-file list would go stale the first time
# someone adds a source file. The tree stays mixed by vintage; what
# changes is that neither vintage can move by accident.
#
# `-text`, never `eol=crlf`. `eol=crlf` implies `text`, which normalizes
# to LF in the object database -- measured: it rewrites a CRLF blob to
# LF on the next commit, so applying it to the eleven produces the same
# 9,751-line rewrite, deliberately. That is what #180 proposes as "the
# narrower option"; it is the damage, not the fix.
#
# This governs git's own conversion. It cannot stop an editor or a
# text-mode script from rewriting a file in place; tests/lineendings.sh
# is what catches that.
*.c -text
*.h -text
# The test scripts, including the one that checks all of this. A shell
# script converted to CRLF does not run: measured, it dies at
# `set: Illegal option -` with exit 2, and ctest reads 2 as a failure
# rather than the 77 the script uses to say it could not run. So on the
# Git for Windows default the guard would be destroyed by the exact
# conversion it exists to catch, and report as a broken test rather than
# an unprotected tree. All ten are unprotected without this line -- the
# nine that existed, and this change's own.
*.sh -text
# sst.doc by name rather than `*.doc`. The reasoning above is about text
# files whose endings must not move; the next `.doc` added here is more
# likely a Word file, which wants `binary`, and inheriting `-text` for it
# would be an accident rather than a decision.
sst.doc -text
